---
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import "./dunstkreis.css";
import "./clocks.css";
import Fig from "../Fig.astro";

const SKY = [
    ["live", "Live sky", "The sky pass every frame and the sky view whenever the Sun moves"],
    ["static", "Static sky", "A cube map written once, drawn by your frame"],
];
const ASKED = [
    [
        "indirect",
        "Indirect light",
        "The sky's light on the scene: the cube map and its coefficients, read by your shading",
    ],
    [
        "direct",
        "Direct light",
        "The Sun's light on the scene, as the meter reads it with the sky view: no pass of its own",
    ],
    [
        "aerial",
        "Aerial perspective",
        "The air in front of the scene, as a volume rebuilt whenever the camera or the Sun moves",
    ],
];
---

<figure class="strip-figure" id="clocks">
    <div class="clocks-bar">
        <span class="steps" role="radiogroup" aria-label="The sky">
            {
                SKY.map(([value, text, title]) => (
                    <label title={title}>
                        <input type="radio" name="clocks-sky" value={value} checked={value === "live"} />
                        <span>{text}</span>
                    </label>
                ))
            }
        </span>
        <span class="steps" role="group" aria-label="What the renderer asks for">
            {
                ASKED.map(([value, text, title]) => (
                    <label title={title}>
                        <input type="checkbox" name="clocks-asked" value={value} checked={value !== "aerial"} />
                        <span>{text}</span>
                    </label>
                ))
            }
        </span>
    </div>
    <div class="strip-panel clocks-panel">
        <svg role="img" aria-label="The pieces of dunstkreis, what each reads, how large it is and when it runs"></svg>
    </div>
    <p class="clocks-measured note" data-field="measured"></p>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        Four pieces, four clocks: every texture and buffer dunstkreis computes, each in the lane of what makes it run
        again. A box shows what it holds, read from this page's own textures and shown as in the figures that explain
        them, the aerial perspective by its last slice, 32 km out, then its size in texels and its memory, textures at
        four half floats per texel. An arrow leads to what reads it, and every pass below the tables reads both, but
        the one computing the coefficients. Dashed, what is yours. The choices light up what runs: a live sky runs the
        sky pass, a static sky is a cube map written once and drawn by your frame; indirect light adds the coefficients;
        direct light costs nothing more, the meter reads it with the sky view. The timings are measured on this GPU as the page loaded, at the sizes the
        tables (<Fig id="tables" />) and the cube map (<Fig id="environment" />) are set to, the sky pass into a full HD
        frame; under the figure, their sums for each occasion.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./clocks";
</script>
